                    span_label:          `Estimated Average Span of Control`,
                    span_hint:           `Estimate DN's CURRENT average span (each director carries 50–150 reports). The healthy target is 6–10 — that gap is the problem you're diagnosing.`,
                    problem_label:       `Primary Structural Problem`,
                    structure_label:     `Proposed Structure Type`,
                    vp_label:            `VP-Level Roles to Create`,
                    layers_label:        `Management Layers (CEO to front-line)`,
                    discount_auth_label: `Authority for Discounts Over 5%`,
                    delegation_label:    `Local Discount Approval Limit (%)`,
                    inventory_auth_label:`Inventory Allocation Authority`,
                    timeline_label:      `Full Rollout Timeline (months)`,
                    budget_label:        `Reorganization Budget (USD)`,
                    change_label:        `Change Management Approach`,
                    submit_btn:          `Submit Round`,
                    undo_btn:            `Undo Last Round`,
                    span_warning:        `Span of control estimate must be between 5 and 200`,
                    structure_warning:   `Please select valid values for all structure fields`,
                    raci_warning:        `Delegation threshold must be between 0% and 10%`,
                    impl_warning:        `Timeline must be between 1 and 18 months`,
                    r1_short:            `Diagnostic`,
                    r2_short:            `Design`,
                    r3_short:            `Governance`,
                    r4_short:            `Implementation`,
                    r1_desc:             `Distribuidora Nacional just lost its largest client, Grupo Retail Centroamericano ($12M annual revenue), because three regional directors gave conflicting responses to a 5% discount request.<br><br>The company has 500 employees across 3 regions (Tegucigalpa: 50, SPS: 150, El Salvador: 120, plus 180 unassigned). Each region is run by a director with 50–150 direct reports and no VP-level specialization. Finance, sales, logistics, and operations are all handled independently per region.<br><br><strong>Your task:</strong> Diagnose the structural problems. Estimate the actual average span of control and identify the root cause of the GRC failure.`,
                    r2_desc:             `Based on your diagnosis, design a new organization for DN. The four options in the case:<br><br><strong>Functional:</strong> national VPs by specialty (sales, logistics, operations).<br><strong>Divisional:</strong> one EVP per region, each running its own functions.<br><strong>Matrix:</strong> each manager reports both to a region and to a function.<br><strong>Hybrid:</strong> functional VPs plus regional coordinators.<br><br>Decide how many VP roles to create (DN has none today; the CFO already exists) and how many management layers the company will have, counting both the CEO level and the front line. Data: 500 employees; a healthy span in distribution is 6 to 10 direct reports per manager.<br><br><em>From this round on, the KPIs show what your design would deliver if it were implemented well. Round 4 shows what the rollout actually achieves.</em>`,
                    r3_desc:             `For the structure you chose, set who decides what (RACI: who is responsible, accountable, consulted and informed). The GRC incident happened because nobody had clear authority over cross-regional pricing.<br><br>Three governance decisions:<br>(1) <strong>Discounts above 5%:</strong> who has the final say?<br>(2) <strong>Local approval limit:</strong> up to what discount can regional supervisors approve without escalating? Data: DN's EBITDA margin is 4.4%, and the same product already sells at prices 10–15% apart across regions.<br>(3) <strong>Inventory allocation:</strong> who decides how stock is split across the three regional depots?<br><br>Authority low enough for speed, escalation where the impact is high: where that line falls is your call.`,
                    r4_desc:             `Plan the transition from today's structure to your design.<br><br><strong>Timeline:</strong> months until the change is complete in every region (1 to 18). Too fast risks chaos and resistance; too slow prolongs the dysfunction that cost GRC. A client with stores in San Pedro Sula and San Salvador wants a pricing meeting before month end.<br><strong>Budget:</strong> the redesign creates about 12 new roles (VPs and regional supervisors). Their salaries come to roughly USD 400,000 a year, plus about USD 50,000 for training and consulting.<br><strong>Approach:</strong> phased pilot (one region first), big bang (all regions at once) or gradual (one function at a time).<br><br>500 employees will see changes in reporting lines, titles and responsibilities.`,
                    hint_high:           `Excellent analysis — your decisions align well with best practices for organizational design.`,
                    hint_medium:         `Good direction. Consider how your choices affect decision speed and cross-regional consistency.`,
                    hint_low:            `Review the case details carefully. Think about what caused the GRC failure and what structural changes would prevent it.`,
